# crash-probe: variables in a `namespace eval` body
#
# 1. A body run from inside a proc.  tclsh gives it its own variable
#    frame: the proc's locals are invisible in it and a bare name is the
#    namespace's variable.  rtcl only switched the current namespace, so
#    the body read and wrote the proc's locals (`set x secret; namespace
#    eval n { set x 1 }` left x at 1) and never saw the namespace's own
#    variables.  The links made in such a body go with its variable
#    frame too: `global` does nothing, `upvar #0` links the namespace,
#    and `upvar 1` to a procedure's variable is refused.
# 2. The namespace chain at the top level: a global written from inside a
#    body by its bare name.  The write reached the global, but its traces
#    did not fire and the array subcommands looked only under the
#    namespace's own name.
# 3. A global that is in the table without a value — a trace on it, a
#    proc's `global`/`upvar #0` link to it, a `variable` declaration.
#    Such an entry takes the write (the namespace's variable used to be
#    made instead), and reading or unsetting it is a different failure
#    from a name nobody knows.
#
# Generated by judge/crash-probes/_gen.tcl from _d27_source.tcl; the
# expected values are what tclsh 8.6.17 gave for these very lines (the
# generator's `--refix` pass).  What is left out, and why, is at the top
# of the source.

source [file join [file dirname [info script]] _chk.tcl]
proc T {script} {
    # A success leaves the previous error's errorCode standing: start
    # each check from NONE.
    set ::errorCode NONE
    set c [catch {uplevel 1 $script} r]
    return [list $c $r [expr {[info exists ::errorCode] ? $::errorCode : {}}]]
}
# A skipped check still builds the state the later ones sit on: its
# script runs, unchecked.
proc U {script} {
    catch {uplevel 1 $script}
}
